  "content": {
    "id": "p49-q10",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "Find the values of $k$ for which these equations have exactly one real solution.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 3,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p49-q10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$kx^2-6x+k=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$k=-3,0,3$. For $k=0$, the equation is linear with one solution.",
          "worked": "For $k\\ne0$, exactly one real solution requires $\\Delta=0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=(-6)^2-4(k)(k)\\\\\\Delta&=36-4k^2\\\\36-4k^2&=0\\\\k^2&=9\\\\k&=\\pm3\\end{align*}$$\nFor $k=0$, the equation becomes $-6x=0$, giving $x=0$, so this also has exactly one real solution.\nTherefore $k=-3,0,3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      },
      {
        "id": "p49-q10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$x^2-(k+4)x+9=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$k=-10$ or $k=2$.",
          "worked": "Exactly one real solution requires $\\Delta=0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=(-(k+4))^2-4(1)(9)\\\\\\Delta&=(k+4)^2-36\\\\(k+4)^2-36&=0\\\\(k+4)^2&=36\\\\k+4&=\\pm6\\\\k&=-10\\text{ or }2\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 55
      },
      {
        "id": "p49-q10-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$2kx^2+2(k-2)x-3=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$k=0$. The equation is then linear; for $k\\ne0$, its discriminant is always positive.",
          "worked": "For $k\\ne0$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=[2(k-2)]^2-4(2k)(-3)\\\\\\Delta&=4(k-2)^2+24k\\\\\\Delta&=4(k^2+2k+4)\\\\\\Delta&=4[(k+1)^2+3]\\end{align*}$$\nThus $\\Delta>0$ for every real $k$, so no quadratic case has exactly one real solution.\nFor $k=0$:\n$$\\begin{align*}-4x-3&=0\\\\-4x&=3\\\\x&=-\\frac34\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore $k=0$ is the only value.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 72
      }
    ]
  },
